\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Four-point lambda dose-response curve. CAR shows a weak positive trend with increasing $\lambda $, but the effect is sublinear (regression slope = 0.75\,pp per 0.01$\lambda $) and the marginal gain at $\lambda {=}0.1$ is not statistically significant. TSR monotonically decreases.}}{6}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:lambda-curve}{{6}{6}{Four-point lambda dose-response curve. CAR shows a weak positive trend with increasing $\lambda $, but the effect is sublinear (regression slope = 0.75\,pp per 0.01$\lambda $) and the marginal gain at $\lambda {=}0.1$ is not statistically significant. TSR monotonically decreases}{table.caption.14}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Per-task breakdown for $\lambda {=}0.1$ (2 seeds, 200 episodes each). The task-specific CAR pattern is preserved (milk $>$ orange\_juice $>$ chocolate\_pudding $>$ bbq\_sauce), consistent with LoRA-only and lower-$\lambda $ conditions.}}{6}{table.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:lambda01-pertask}{{7}{6}{Per-task breakdown for $\lambda {=}0.1$ (2 seeds, 200 episodes each). The task-specific CAR pattern is preserved (milk $>$ orange\_juice $>$ chocolate\_pudding $>$ bbq\_sauce), consistent with LoRA-only and lower-$\lambda $ conditions}{table.caption.15}{}}
